@page "/graph-plugins"

<CatHead Title="Graph plugins" Tier="@CatHead.Tier3">
    What a graph does beyond drawing and reading: a minimap for a large one, links the reader draws, groups
    that fold and groups that are outlined, the drawing as a file, and two more layouts. Each loads the first
    time a graph on the page uses it, from inside the package, so a page that uses none downloads none. How a
    reader moves through a graph is on <a href="/graph-exploring">Exploring a graph</a>.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphPlugins.Minimap" Title="The minimap" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; zoom in on one domain, then press or drag in the minimap.</strong>
        <code>&lt;canvas class="graph-minimap" data-graph-minimap aria-hidden="true"&gt;</code>, written inside the
        graph, draws the whole drawing in miniature and frames the part in view. Pressing it moves the view there,
        dragging pans, and the wheel zooms about the point under the pointer. It is Sedna.UI's own, in the canvas's
        colours, and while the view pans only the frame is drawn again.
    </p>
    <p>
        Write one when a graph is larger than its frame, so that zooming in on a part does not lose the whole. It is
        hidden on a phone, where the frame is the whole screen, and from assistive technology: the keyboard already
        pans and zooms, with <kbd class="kbd">Shift</kbd> + arrows, <kbd class="kbd">+</kbd>
        <kbd class="kbd">−</kbd> and <kbd class="kbd">0</kbd> to fit.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphPlugins.MinimapCorners" Title="Where the minimap sits" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        Bottom right by default. <code>.graph-minimap--start</code> moves it to the start edge and
        <code>.graph-minimap--top</code> to the top, and the two combine. Keep it clear of the tools:
        <code>.graph-tools--stack</code> stands them in a column, <code>.graph-tools--bottom</code> moves them to
        the bottom and <code>.graph-tools--start</code> to the start edge. In a <code>.graph--sm</code> the minimap
        is smaller.
    </p>
    <p>
        The two graphs read one list: <code>data-graph-data="#pl-bike-parts"</code> on a graph names a
        <code>.graph-data</code> list anywhere on the page, so two views of the same records share one text
        alternative.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphPlugins.DrawLinks" Title="Drawing links" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; turn drawing on and give Campaign results a feed.</strong> The <code>connect</code>
        toggle &mdash; or <code>data-graph-connect</code> on the graph, to start drawing &mdash; turns a drag from
        one record to another into a link, and the graph dispatches <code>sedna-graph-connect</code> with
        <code>{ source, target }</code>. While it is on, the graph carries <code>data-graph-drawing</code> and the
        pointer is a crosshair; a toggle's two icons are <code>.graph-when-off</code> and
        <code>.graph-when-on</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>The line drawn is removed again, and the app adds the link to its own data</strong>, the way a drop
        in <a href="/drag-drop">drag and drop</a> is the app's to make: it can check it, save it, or refuse it. This
        page has no app behind it, so <code>data-graph-managed</code> keeps what is drawn instead. In Blazor the
        handler is <code>@@onsedna-graph-connect</code> with <code>SednaGraphConnectEventArgs</code>, on
        <a href="/graph-blazor">Graph in Blazor</a>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="GraphPlugins/Connect.html" Title="Keeping a drawn link without Blazor">
    <p>
        The event is an ordinary bubbling DOM event. Save the link, then add an item to the list: the list is the
        data, so a new item is a new line on the canvas.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphPlugins.FoldGroups" Title="Folding groups" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; open every module, or right-click one.</strong> A record with <code>data-parent</code> is
        drawn inside that record, which becomes a group. <code>data-graph-collapse</code> gives every group a fold
        cue in its corner while it is selected &mdash; click a group, then its cue &mdash; and
        <code>data-collapsed</code> on a group starts it folded, as Shared is here. A folded group is one record, and the links of what is inside it end on it.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>expand-all</code> and <code>collapse-all</code> act on every group. <code>expand</code> and
        <code>collapse</code> act on one &mdash; a button's <code>value</code>, else the menu's record, else the
        selection &mdash; and in a menu each hides itself where it does not apply. Folding dispatches
        <code>sedna-graph-collapse</code> and unfolding <code>sedna-graph-expand</code>, each with
        <code>{ id, label }</code>. An unfolded group opens where it is and the others stay put;
        <code>arrange</code> lays the drawing out again.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphPlugins.Outlines" Title="Outlining groups" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> <code>data-graph-hulls</code> &mdash; or the <code>hulls</code> toggle &mdash; draws a
        soft outline around every <code>data-group</code> of two or more records, in the group's tone and routed
        around the records that are not in it. The records are laid out by their links, so the outline is how the
        reader sees who owns what on a map shaped by something else. It follows a record that is dragged, and
        fades while a record is lit.
    </p>
    <p>
        A group's key is <code>.graph-swatch--group</code> in its <code>.series-*</code> colour. Its tone is its
        heart's &mdash; the record whose id is the group's name &mdash; else the one most of its members wear.
        A <code>data-group</code> is not a container: for records drawn inside another, use
        <code>data-parent</code>, as in the two examples either side of this one.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphPlugins.Export" Title="Exporting the drawing" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; both buttons download.</strong> <code>export-svg</code> and <code>export-png</code> save
        the whole drawing &mdash; every record shown, not only the part in view &mdash; in the colours of the theme
        it is shown in, so a dark page exports dark. <code>data-graph-filename</code> on the button names the file;
        without it the file is named after the graph's id.
    </p>
    <p>
        The SVG is Sedna.UI's own: each record a group with a <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>, the text as text, and every
        colour the one the canvas resolved from the tokens. The PNG is the engine's, at twice the resolution. From
        script, <code>graph.export('svg')</code> resolves a <code>Blob</code>; from C#, <code>ExportAsync</code>
        returns a <code>data:</code> URL and <code>DownloadAsync</code> saves the file.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphPlugins.Hierarchy" Title="Hierarchies" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>data-graph-layout="dagre"</code> lays a graph out in ranks &mdash; an org chart, a flow, a dependency
        tree &mdash; in the <code>data-graph-direction</code> given: <code>TB</code>, the default, <code>LR</code>,
        <code>BT</code> or <code>RL</code>. Boxes and <code>data-graph-curve="taxi"</code> draw the right angles of an
        org chart. Every layout, and switching between them, is on <a href="/graph-layouts">Graph layouts</a>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphPlugins.NestedSprings" Title="Nested springs" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>data-graph-layout="fcose"</code> is springs that also lay out groups inside groups: hosts in racks,
        racks in a room, all from <code>data-parent</code>. Boxes keep every name inside its group's
        edge, which suits a diagram of a few dozen records. More on <a href="/graph-layouts">Graph layouts</a>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<h2 id="plugins">What loads, and under which licence <CatAnchor Id="plugins" /></h2>
<p>
    Everything below ships inside the package under <code>_content/Sedna.UI/lib/cytoscape/</code>, with each
    licence beside the files it covers in <code>licenses/</code>. Nothing is fetched from anywhere else, and a
    plugin that fails to load is reported in the console while the graph goes on drawing. The pinned versions are
    in <code>VENDORED.txt</code> there and in <code>THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md</code>.
</p>
<div class="sedna-scroll-x">
    <table class="table">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>For</th><th>Package</th><th>Licence</th><th>Loaded</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>The canvas, and the PNG export</td><td>cytoscape</td><td>MIT</td><td>With the first graph on a page.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The minimap, the SVG export</td><td>Sedna.UI's own</td><td>Apache-2.0</td><td>With the graph module, which the first graph brings.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Drawing links</td><td>cytoscape-edgehandles, with lodash.memoize and lodash.throttle</td><td>MIT</td><td>When drawing is first turned on.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Folding groups</td><td>cytoscape-expand-collapse</td><td>MIT</td><td>For a graph with <code>data-graph-collapse</code> or a <code>data-collapsed</code> group, or at the first fold.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Outlining groups</td><td>cytoscape-bubblesets, with bubblesets-js, cytoscape-layers and lodash.throttle</td><td>MIT</td><td>When outlines are first turned on.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Hierarchies</td><td>cytoscape-dagre, which bundles @@dagrejs/dagre and @@dagrejs/graphlib</td><td>MIT</td><td>When the <code>dagre</code> layout first runs.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Nested springs</td><td>cytoscape-fcose, with cose-base and layout-base</td><td>MIT</td><td>When the <code>fcose</code> layout first runs.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<div class="callout callout-info">
    <i class="ri-scales-3-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">Left out on purpose: cytoscape-svg</span>
        It exports SVG as well, and it is licensed under GPL-3.0, which an Apache-2.0 package cannot carry without
        passing its terms on to every app that installs it. The SVG export is Sedna.UI's own code instead, and a
        copyleft plugin is out of scope whatever it offers.
    </div>
</div>

<h2 id="actions">Actions, events and calls <CatAnchor Id="actions" /></h2>
<div class="sedna-scroll-x">
    <table class="table">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Plugin</th><th>On the graph</th><th>Action</th><th>Event</th><th>C#</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Minimap</td><td><code>canvas[data-graph-minimap]</code> inside it</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Drawing links</td><td><code>data-graph-connect</code>, <code>data-graph-managed</code></td><td><code>connect</code></td><td><code>sedna-graph-connect</code></td><td><code>SetDrawingAsync</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Folding groups</td><td><code>data-graph-collapse</code>; <code>data-collapsed</code> on a group</td><td><code>expand</code>, <code>collapse</code>, <code>expand-all</code>, <code>collapse-all</code></td><td><code>sedna-graph-expand</code>, <code>sedna-graph-collapse</code></td><td><code>ExpandAsync</code>, <code>CollapseAsync</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Outlining groups</td><td><code>data-graph-hulls</code></td><td><code>hulls</code></td><td>&mdash;</td><td><code>SetHullsAsync</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Export</td><td>&mdash;</td><td><code>export-svg</code>, <code>export-png</code>, with <code>data-graph-filename</code></td><td>&mdash;</td><td><code>ExportAsync</code>, <code>DownloadAsync</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Hierarchies, nested springs</td><td><code>data-graph-layout="dagre"</code>, <code>"fcose"</code></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td><code>LayoutAsync</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>
    From script, the handle <code>sednaUi.graph.get(id)</code> resolves has <code>connect(on)</code>,
    <code>collapse(id?)</code>, <code>expand(id?)</code>, <code>hulls(on)</code>, <code>export(format)</code> and
    <code>download(format, filename)</code>.
</p>
